---
// About this site: how it relates to Enterprise Blueprints, for its visitors, and,
// until you make the site your own, how to do that. This page is yours to change.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LibraryMark from '../components/LibraryMark.astro';
import { elementsOfType, inUse, isOwn, ownPackages, release, upstream } from '../lib/library';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { site } from '../site';

const library = upstream!;
const publisher = ownPackages[0]?.publisher.name ?? site.title;
const libraryMissions = elementsOfType('mission').filter((m) => !isOwn(m.package) && inUse(m.package));
const catalog = new URL(href('catalog.json'), Astro.site).href;
---
<Base title="About" description={`How ${site.title} relates to ${library.title}, and what it adds.`}>
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>About {site.title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">{site.description}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Built on {library.title}</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={library.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{library.title}<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        is an open reference library of public-sector missions: what public organizations are responsible
        for, the outcomes they seek, the capabilities and requirements that work depends on, and example
        patterns that might realize them, each citing its sources. It's the shared knowledge this site
        builds on, and it includes no one's products or services.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site adds {publisher}’s own work to it, described in the library's terms: each offering names
        the missions, capabilities, and patterns it helps with. It builds on library release {library.release}.
      </p>

      <h2>Finding your way</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The library's missions</strong>{site.upstream?.showAll ? ', all of them at this release,' : " this site's work builds on"}{libraryMissions.length > 0 && <>, such as{' '}
          <a href={libraryMissions[0].url}>{libraryMissions[0].title}</a>,</>} have their pages here, with what
          we offer shown beside the items it helps with.{site.upstream?.showAll && <> A switch on each list narrows
          it to what this site's work builds on.</>}
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A small book</strong>{libraryMissions.length > 0 && <> (<LibraryMark element={libraryMissions[0]} legend />)</>}
          marks items from {library.title}. Each of their pages links to the original in the library, which has
          the newest release.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Everything else is ours:</strong> our offerings, and any missions, capabilities, or patterns of
          our own.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Using it in your tools</h2>
      <p>
        Our packages use the library's own format. Tools that read the library, such as Solution Studio, can
        read ours beside it from our catalog at <code>{catalog}</code>. This is release {release}.
      </p>

      <section class="notice make-it-yours" aria-labelledby="yours-heading">
        <h2 id="yours-heading">Making this site yours</h2>
        <p>
          This part is for the team that runs the site. It comes with the Blueprint Kit, so delete it once the
          site is yours.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Name the site</strong> and choose its color in <code>kit.yaml</code>.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Describe what you offer</strong> in the browser with <code>pnpm editor</code>, or by hand in
            <code>source/offerings/resources.yaml</code>: services, products, demos, templates, training, or case
            studies, each naming the library items it helps with. Add missions or capabilities of your own in
            their own package if the library doesn't cover them yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Build and check:</strong> <code>pnpm content:build</code>, then <code>pnpm verify</code>,
            which checks every link against the library.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make the pages yours:</strong> the home page is <code>src/pages/index.astro</code> and this
            page is <code>src/pages/about.astro</code>. The rest of the pages are the library's, and
            <code>pnpm sync --tools</code> brings them up to date when you choose.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Publish:</strong> push to GitHub with Pages turned on, and each week a pull request brings in
            the library's newest release and the kit's latest pages, for you to review and merge. Or run
            <code>pnpm content:publish</code> and host the built site anywhere you like.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>The kit's README covers each step in detail.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .make-it-yours { margin-top: 2.5rem; max-width: 54rem; }
  .make-it-yours h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  .make-it-yours ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 1em; }
  .make-it-yours li { margin-bottom: .45em; }
</style>
